[2.78521, "o", "\r\n"]
[30.034329, "o", "The user wants a function that checks if a word is a palindrome. Let me write a Lex function for this. I'll check the string stdlib for reversing or use a simple approach.\r\n\r\n"]
[31.105816, "o", "Let me write a palindrome function. I need to check if a string equals its reverse. Let me look at the str module to find how to reverse a string.\r\n"]
[31.105926, "o", "\r\n[tool: lex_stdlib]\r\n"]
[31.119029, "o", "[running: lex_stdlib]\r\n[ok]\r\n"]
[39.279565, "o", "Let me write an is_palindrome function. I'll split into characters, reverse, and compare. str.join and str.split with empty separator.\r\n"]
[39.279718, "o", "\r\n[tool: write]\r\n"]
[39.467702, "o", "[running: write]\r\n[ok]\r\n"]
[42.186758, "o", "The check passed. Let me run the examples to confirm.\r\n"]
[42.186863, "o", "\r\n[tool: lex_run]\r\n"]
[42.262623, "o", "[running: lex_run]\r\n[ok]\r\n"]
[45.986744, "o", "Let me run a test by passing args.The `lex check` passed and the examples compiled fine. The runtime error is just because I invoked the function without arguments (it needs a word). Let me confirm it runs correctly with a value:\r\n"]
[45.98686, "o", "\r\n[tool: write]\r\n"]
[46.019498, "o", "[running: write]\r\n[error]\r\n"]
[49.901989, "o", "The example test cases already validated the function, so the demo file is just clutter. Let me remove it and rely on the examples to verify everything works.\r\n"]
[49.902106, "o", "\r\n[tool: bash]\r\n"]
[49.915466, "o", "[running: bash]\r\n[ok]\r\n"]
[50.822147, "o", "Let me just verify with a test.\r\n"]
[50.822176, "o", "\r\n[tool: lex_test]\r\n"]
[50.828755, "o", "[running: lex_test]\r\n"]
[50.828815, "o", "[ok]\r\n"]
[52.705213, "o", "The lex_run had an arity issue, probably it just requires args. Let me just run with an argument.\r\n"]
[52.705298, "o", "\r\n[tool: lex_run]\r\n"]
[52.714481, "o", "[running: lex_run]\r\n"]
[52.714505, "o", "[ok]\r\n"]
[53.970674, "o", "It works. The earlier error was because I ran it without an argument.Done. The function `is_palindrome` in `src/palindrome.lex`:\r\n\r\n"]
[54.031453, "o", "```lex\r\n"]
[54.226973, "o", "fn is_palindrome(word :: Str) -> Bool\r\n"]
[54.296715, "o", "  examples {\r\n"]
[54.465072, "o", "    is_palindrome(\"radar\") => true,\r\n"]
[54.605652, "o", "    is_palindrome(\"Racecar\") => true,\r\n"]
[54.746254, "o", "    is_palindrome(\"hello\") => false,\r\n"]
[54.983053, "o", "    is_palindrome(\"\") => true\r\n"]
[54.983437, "o", "   }\r\n"]
[55.054665, "o", "{\r\n"]
[55.19607, "o", "  let lower := str.to_lower(word)\r\n"]
[55.410324, "o", "  let reversed := str.join(list.reverse(str.split(lower, \"\")), \"\")\r\n"]
[55.480359, "o", "  lower == reversed\r\n"]
[55.480631, "o", "}\r\n"]
[55.480867, "o", "```\r\n\r\n"]
[56.600585, "o", "It's a pure function, type-checks clean, and passes its examples (verified `is_palindrome(\"radar\") => true`). It lowercases the word first, so matching is case-insensitive — e.g. `\"Racecar\"` also returns `true`.\r\n\r\n"]
[57.320645, "o", "The earlier `lex run` panic was just because I invoked it without the string argument; passing `\"radar\"` confirms it returns `true`.\r\n"]
[57.320673, "o", "\r\n"]
[57.321753, "o", "\r\n(trail: .lex/sessions/cli-1790251703662-4b7b9990.db)\r\n\r\n"]
